 Byte-level language models are motivated by their ability to operate directly on raw bytes without relying on subword tokenization. To assess whether this advantage is preserved in practice, we evaluate the models along two complementary dimensions: general performance and robustness to input perturbations.
 
 \textbf{General Performance.}
-We assess general modeling capability using bits per byte (BPB), standard downstream accuracy, inference latency, and retrieval accuracy. BPB is measured on a fixed held-out DCLM validation split ($320$ non-overlapping $3{,}840$-byte windows), normalizing every model's log-likelihood by the same byte count; Appendix~\ref{app:bpb} details the computation and additionally reports the training-EMA readout at the iso-data endpoint, which preserves the same model ordering. For downstream evaluation, we report mean zero-, three-, and five-shot accuracy across HellaSwag~\cite{zellers2019hellaswag}, ARC-Easy, ARC-Challenge~\cite{arc}, PIQA~\cite{PIQA}, and WinoGrande~\cite{ai2:winogrande}.\footnote{BoolQ~\cite{clark2019boolq} and MMLU~\cite{mmlu} are also evaluated, but excluded here because some few-shot examples exceed the models' context length.} We also evaluate retrieval accuracy using \textit{Needle-in-a-Haystack} (NIAH) tasks~\cite{kamradt2023needle}. Following the protocols of~\citet{hsieh2024ruler} while aligning the context size of the trained models, we report averaged accuracy on Single NIAH (S-NIAH-1/2/3). 
+We assess general modeling capability using bits per byte (BPB), standard downstream accuracy, inference latency, and retrieval accuracy. BPB is measured on a fixed held-out DCLM validation split ($320$ non-overlapping $3{,}840$-byte windows), normalizing every model's log-likelihood by the same byte count; Appendix~\ref{app:bpb} details the computation and additionally reports the training-EMA readout at the iso-data endpoint, which preserves the same model ordering. For downstream evaluation, we report mean zero-, three-, and five-shot accuracy across HellaSwag~\cite{zellers2019hellaswag}, ARC-Easy, ARC-Challenge~\cite{arc}, PIQA~\cite{PIQA}, and WinoGrande~\cite{ai2:winogrande}.\footnote{BoolQ~\cite{clark2019boolq} and MMLU~\cite{mmlu} are also evaluated, but excluded here because some few-shot examples exceed the models' context length.} \hyun{We additionally report zero-shot LAMBADA~\cite{lambada}, which asks for the last word of a passage; an item counts as correct only if the model's greedy prediction reproduces the whole target word, so subword and byte-level models are scored on the identical string.} We also evaluate retrieval accuracy using \textit{Needle-in-a-Haystack} (NIAH) tasks~\cite{kamradt2023needle}. Following the protocols of~\citet{hsieh2024ruler} while aligning the context size of the trained models, we report averaged accuracy on Single NIAH (S-NIAH-1/2/3). 
 % \hyun{Each item hides a single key--value needle at a random depth of a haystack and asks the model to reproduce the value verbatim: S-NIAH-1 hides a numeric value in a repeated-noise haystack, whereas S-NIAH-2 and S-NIAH-3 hide a $7$-digit key and a UUID in natural DCLM text, with $50$ items per context length. The single-key template of \citet{hsieh2024ruler} separates the query from its value with a colon, which subword tokenizers absorb into a single unit but byte-level models do not; we therefore score a colon-free variant of the template for every model, and Appendix~\ref{app:protocol} details this boundary-consistent format and quantifies the artifact it removes.} 
 To assess computational efficiency, we measure inference latency as the average forward-pass time, in seconds, over inputs containing the same number of text bytes.
 
