 \label{sec:results}
 \paragraph{General Performance.}
 Table~\ref{tab:main_13b} summarizes the comparison at the 1B-parameter scale. Among the three models trained under matched settings, the Transformer achieves the lowest held-out BPB ($0.886$), followed by BPEByte ($0.908$) and AUNet ($0.936$). Despite these differences in language-modeling quality, the models perform similarly in the zero-shot setting. \hyun{This phenomenon has also been observed in prior work, suggesting that pretraining loss alone does not fully explain downstream performance.~\citep{liu2023sameNPTBD, tay2023scaling,marathe2026breaking}.}
-In the few-shot settings, BPEByte, together with AUNet, achieves higher average accuracy than Transformer. 
-For external reference, \hyun{H-Net, trained on $460$B bytes, reaches accuracy similar to that of the matched models, whereas BLT, trained on roughly $1$T bytes, is higher at every shot count ($63.3$, $66.2$, and $66.6$; Appendix~\ref{app:blt}). Neither is trained under our matched budget, so these rows are references rather than controlled comparisons.}
+In the few-shot settings, BPEByte, together with AUNet, achieves higher average accuracy than Transformer. \hyun{On LAMBADA, both byte-level models predict the final word more accurately than the Transformer (BPEByte $64.8$ and AUNet $64.6$ vs.\ $62.7$; paired differences $+2.1$ and $+2.0$ points, $95\%$ CIs $[0.9, 3.3]$ and $[0.7, 3.2]$).}
+For external reference, \hyun{H-Net, trained on $460$B bytes, reaches accuracy similar to that of the matched models on the multiple-choice tasks but trails them on LAMBADA ($48.5$, in line with the $48.4$ reported for this checkpoint~\citep{HNet}), whereas BLT, trained on roughly $1$T bytes, is higher at every shot count ($63.3$, $66.2$, and $66.6$; Appendix~\ref{app:blt}). Neither is trained under our matched budget, so these rows are references rather than controlled comparisons.}
 Regarding retrieval performance, \hyun{BPEByte achieves the highest average accuracy at $0.99$, followed by the subword Transformer and BLT at $0.98$,} H-Net at $0.94$, \hyun{and AUNet at} $0.85$. BPEByte's advantage \hyun{over AUNet and H-Net} is driven primarily by its stable performance on S-NIAH-3, which requires exact retrieval of a UUID---a formatted string rather than natural-language text.
 
 
